{# "What do these statuses mean?" — the answer, in the place where the user
   meets the badge, not only behind a threshold-gated recommendation callout.

   This exists because the original page showed five amber "Unknown" pills at
   100% DKIM and 100% SPF with an Authorize button and no explanation of what
   Unknown meant, whether action was expected, or how to decide. All three
   questions are answered here.

   Opens by default while any sender is still awaiting review, because that is
   exactly when a first-time reader needs it. Collapsed once everything is
   decided so it stops taking space.

   Props:
     - needsReviewCount  (int)  how many senders are still awaiting a decision
#}
{% props needsReviewCount = 0 %}

<details class="collapse collapse-arrow border border-base-200 bg-base-100 rounded-box mb-4"
         data-testid="sender-status-legend"
         {{ needsReviewCount > 0 ? 'open' : '' }}>
    <summary class="collapse-title text-sm font-medium min-h-0 py-3">
        What do Authorized, Needs review and Not authorized mean?
    </summary>
    <div class="collapse-content text-sm text-base-content/70 space-y-3">
        <p>
            Every server that sends mail claiming to be your domain shows up here — your own mail host, your
            newsletter tool, your invoicing system, and anyone spoofing you. Sendvery cannot tell those apart for
            you, so each one carries a status you own.
        </p>

        <ul class="space-y-2">
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                <span class="badge badge-sm badge-success shrink-0 mt-0.5">Authorized</span>
                <span>You confirmed this server is allowed to send as your domain. It drops out of alerts and reminders.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                <span class="badge badge-sm badge-warning shrink-0 mt-0.5">Needs review</span>
                <span>
                    We have seen it, but nobody has decided about it yet. <strong>This is the one that wants something from you.</strong>
                </span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                <span class="badge badge-sm badge-error shrink-0 mt-0.5">Not authorized</span>
                <span>You looked at it and said it is not yours. It stays flagged so it cannot quietly become normal.</span>
            </li>
        </ul>

        <div>
            <p class="font-semibold text-base-content">How do I verify a sender is really mine?</p>
            <p class="mt-1">
                Three signals together are strong evidence. Take
                <code class="font-mono text-xs bg-base-200 px-1 rounded">mxb.seznam.cz</code> at 100% DKIM and 100% SPF:
            </p>
            <ol class="list-decimal list-inside mt-1 space-y-1">
                <li><strong>You recognise the name.</strong> Seznam is your mail provider, or your newsletter tool, or your CRM — something you pay for.</li>
                <li><strong>DKIM and SPF pass close to 100%.</strong> A spoofer cannot sign with your DKIM key. Consistently passing DKIM means whoever runs that server holds your key.</li>
                <li><strong>The volume matches mail you actually send.</strong> If your team sends a few hundred messages a week, a sender at that scale is plausible; one at 50,000 is not.</li>
            </ol>
            <p class="mt-2">
                If all three hold, authorize it. If you do not recognise the name <em>and</em> its mail is failing,
                mark it not authorized. If you are unsure, leave it — see below.
            </p>
        </div>

        <div>
            <p class="font-semibold text-base-content">What happens if I do nothing?</p>
            <p class="mt-1">
                Nothing breaks. Statuses are notes to yourself — they never change your DNS, SPF, DKIM or DMARC
                policy, and they never block or release a single message. What actually stops spoofed mail is your
                DMARC policy. Leaving senders in Needs review only means Sendvery keeps flagging them here, in your
                alerts and in your weekly email, because it has no way of knowing they are fine.
            </p>
        </div>

        <p>
            <a href="{{ path('knowledge_base_article', { slug: 'authorizing-senders-explained' }) }}"
               class="link link-primary"
               data-testid="sender-legend-kb-link">Read the full guide to authorizing senders →</a>
        </p>
    </div>
</details>
